Delegation chain walker + replay-defense conformance (#66)

* Add multi-hop delegation walker + replay-defense conformance

lib/delegation-chain.ts implements §3 of chain-replay.pv: each hop
signs hopBind(nonce, prev_id, next_id, message_hash) under Ed25519.
The walker verifies depth-N chains with a nonce-freshness table and
rejects splices, replays, hop-swaps, and chain truncation.

Conformance test exercises the counter-traces the .pv defends against:
nonce reuse, hop swap, signature forgery, chain truncation. 17/17 pass.

Updates RUNTIME-CONFORMANCE.md to drop the SPEC-ONLY tag on delegation.

Spec: proofs/anchor/delegation/chain-replay.pv
Runtime: lib/delegation-chain.ts

* fix(delegation): bind signer identity to chain identity + add hopBind DST

Addresses three findings from the adversarial security review on PR #66
(comment #4488401165):

F1 — Verifier accepted attacker-supplied signing key (confidence 5).
  The DelegationHop carried both prevId (chain identity) and kid
  (verification key), and verifyDelegationChain used kid to load the
  Ed25519 public key while building the signed payload from prevId.
  signHop set them equal, but the verifier trusted whatever the network
  sent. Attack: Eve crafts a hop with prevId=idVictim, kid=idEve, signed
  under skEve — verifier accepts as Victim's delegation. Breaks the .pv
  proof's chain_accepted ==> chain_authorized invariant including hop[0]
  (principal impersonation).

  Fix: deleted kid entirely. prevId is now both the chain-identity field
  AND the verification-key source — they cannot drift apart by
  construction.

F2 — Tests didn't cover F1 (confidence 5). The existing signature-tamper
  test only flipped a sig byte (trivially broken signature). The 17 tests
  as written would have all passed even against the broken verifier.
  Added four new tests:
   - (F2a) honest hop1 + Eve-signed hop with prevId=idVictim → rejected
   - (F2b) hop[0] principal impersonation variant → rejected
   - (F3) signature over a v0-style payload (no DST) → rejected
   - (F3-meta) HOP_BIND_DST literally appears as the first key in the
     canonical signed bytes

F3 — Missing domain separation in the signed payload (confidence 4).
  The .pv writes hopBind(...) : bitstring [data] — a typed constructor,
  collision-free by construction. The TS encoded a plain JSON object
  {messageHash, nextId, nonce, prevId} with no type discriminator. Any
  other signer in the codebase that ever signs an object with these four
  keys becomes a confused-deputy oracle.

  Fix: hopBindBytes now prepends _dst: 'anchor.delegation.hopBind.v1' as
  the first canonical key. Exported HOP_BIND_DST so consumers (and the
  test) can reference the value. Bumping the version is an intentional
  hard wire-break for any future protocol change.

Deferred (acknowledged but out of scope for this commit):
F4 NonceTable is in-memory/unbounded (DoS + lost replay protection
  across daemon restarts). Tracked separately — needs the SQLite-backed
  table design.
F5 Truncation isn't enforced (verifier accepts any chain length ≥ 1).
  The runtime is correctly scoped to no splice / no replay / no
  message substitution. Minimum-depth / required-intermediary is an
  application-policy concern that should be added as a verifier param
  in a separate change; RUNTIME-CONFORMANCE.md should be updated to
  reflect the actual scope.

All 21 tests pass (was 17, added 4 for F2+F3). Typecheck clean.

